A||||9||Wolfhound||windsield removal||||5||
Z||000000||Wolfhound||02-25-2001||06:06 AM||||I picked up an 86 SE 2.8 auto yesterday with a busted windshield. I have a none fixable parts car with a good one. <BR>Anyone here have any experience removing a windshield intact? wire saw adhesive maybe?<BR>Any info appreciated!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||02-25-2001||11:17 AM||||There is a tool that has a handle with an "L" shaped blade on it and a cable with another handle. You put the blade between glass and frame, twist so blade is under glass and pull the cable handle around. This works so much better than the wire saw. The tool costs about $14 and you can get a windshield out in about 15 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||02-25-2001||11:48 AM||||pull out the rubber moulding strip first that locks the glass in.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||02-25-2001||11:59 AM||||you CAN use a razor knife all the way around the windshield to slice thru the urethane adhesive and the windshield will simply lift off, that's the way we remove them here at the shop.........takes a little time and care but it works.......start at the top and work your way down the sides finishing with the bottom and then just tilt the windshield forward and lift it off......||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||02-25-2001||12:17 PM||||Thanks ,I have the molding removed and if this rain gets out of here I,ll give it a shot.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||02-25-2001||12:22 PM||||Also, Be VERY careful. One little chip on the edge of the windshield and it will turn into a crack over time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-25-2001||04:10 PM||||Make sure any knicks in the edge are sanded out with aluminum oxide sand paper. That prevents them starting cracks. They don't have to be pretty. just take off the sharp edges. Use 220 and finer paper. preferably wet.<P>Also, you want to try to work where it's warm. They break easier if they are cold.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||02-25-2001||07:55 PM||||Piano Wire or a small guitar wire does the trick every time.  I've used this method on quite a few windshields.  Beware of using too thick of wire because the windshield tends to crack.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||02-25-2001||08:32 PM||||I removed some of the broken windshield this afternoon. I see now what I,m up against. I think removing the good one will be easier.<BR>I will get a removal tool, and guitar (little E),urathane adhesive,and 220 paper in the morning. Fabricated a tool but the metal wasn't strong enough.<BR>Thanks Guys ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wolfhound||02-26-2001||04:13 PM||||Well, Thanks to everyones advice I was able to pull this off. I managed to borrow the tools I needed from a friend and it went pretty smooth. <BR> Oreif, He had the tool you described and it was perfect for removing the old windshield, but I was afaid to use it on the good one as it wanted to chip the edges a little.<BR> tgowens, He had a razor knife with a 24 inch extesion handle. I used that to remove the good glass. If someone made longer blades it would have been a snap. Took almost more patients than I had availiable. I was a nervious wreck by the time I got it out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>here are a couple of photos as before and after.<BR>Opal, dropped a tree on this car in Oct.1995<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4.JPG"> <BR>This is an 86 V6 SE automatic with sub woofer. It has 18,400 miles on the odometer, a real cinderella.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/aftr.JPG"> <BR>Thanks all<BR>||||reg||7
